{
  "schema": 3,
  "description": "Batch 8: tool identity, path escape, structure, non-ASCII, state and destructive-but-not-delete verbs. The first six rounds all attacked the command string; this round stops assuming the danger is in the string. Findings: the entry layer used to wash a hard deny white whenever the tool name was unrecognised (fixed by letting a rules-layer deny outrank an entry-layer ask); fullwidth latin, zero-width space, soft hyphen, tabs and NUL all bypassed the head check until canonicalisation moved to the front of the pipeline; device paths, git history rewrites and package registry overrides were unguarded. C8 is pinned at ask on purpose: the third nesting level loses its -c flag in tokenisation, so the command is never reconstructed, and it does not execute as a recursive delete.",
